Declares the resource's launch tool arguments: the tool-invocation prefix that hosts the program, for example run ./cmd/api for go, -m flask for python, or tool exec <package> --yes -- for dotnet.

public static class ResourceBuilderExtensions
{
    public static IResourceBuilder<T> WithLaunchToolArgs<T>(
        this IResourceBuilder<T> builder,
        Action<CommandLineArgsCallbackContext> callback,
        string? ownedByLaunchConfigurationType = null,
        bool showInCommandLine = true)
    {
        // ...
    }
}

Parameters

• builder (IResourceBuilder (https://aspire.dev/reference/api/csharp/aspire.hosting/iresourcebuilder-1.md))                                                                                                                                                                            
  The resource builder.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                
• callback (Action<CommandLineArgsCallbackContext>)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
  Callback that produces the launch tool arguments. It is invoked with an empty CommandLineArgsCallbackContext.Args (https://aspire.dev/reference/api/csharp/aspire.hosting/commandlineargscallbackcontext/properties.md#args) list; everything it adds becomes the leading arguments. 
• ownedByLaunchConfigurationType (string?) optional                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
  The debug launch configuration type that performs this tool invocation itself, for example "go" or "python" -- the same value passed to WithDebugSupport. Leave this null (the default) when the prefix is not a debugging concern and must always be passed to the launched program.
• showInCommandLine (bool) optional                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    
  Whether these arguments are part of the command line shown for the resource in the dashboard. Pass false for a prefix that is pure invocation plumbing the user did not write and cannot act on.                                                                                     

Returns

IResourceBuilder (https://aspire.dev/reference/api/csharp/aspire.hosting/iresourcebuilder-1.md) -- A reference to the ApplicationModel.IResourceBuilder1`.

Remarks

Launch tool arguments are always placed ahead of every argument contributed by WithArgs, no matter when this method is called, and no WithArgs callback can observe or modify them. Declaring the tool invocation this way therefore avoids any dependency on the order in which resource builder methods are called.

When ownedByLaunchConfigurationType is supplied and the resource is launched by an IDE using a launch configuration of that type (see WithDebugSupport), the launch configuration already carries the tool invocation, so these arguments are not passed to the launched program. In every other case -- process execution, publish, or when a launch configuration of a different type is active -- they are part of the resource's command line.

This has no effect on a container resource, which invokes its program through the image's ENTRYPOINT rather than through a local tool. That matters for executables published as a Dockerfile, because ExecutableResourceBuilderExtensions.PublishAsDockerFile(IResourceBuilder) (https://aspire.dev/reference/api/csharp/aspire.hosting/executableresourcebuilderextensions/methods.md#publishasdockerfile-iresourcebuilder-t) carries the executable's annotations over to the generated container resource.

Calling this method more than once is allowed; the most recent declaration wins. It therefore declares a single tool invocation rather than accumulating one, which is why it is the integration's own description of how it launches the program, not a place for callers to append flags.

